    lesson: 'The course closes on one sentence, and its structure is the teaching: "If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men" (Romans 12:18). Paul puts TWO LIMITS in before he asks for anything, and then removes every limit from the part most people would want qualified. IF IT BE POSSIBLE. Scripture concedes that peace is sometimes not available. That is not resignation and it is not a loophole — it is accuracy, and it matters pastorally, because a teacher who deletes it leaves a learner reading every failed relationship as his own sin when it may simply have been another person’s choice. Some tenancies end badly and you did not cause it. AS MUCH AS LIETH IN YOU. The portion is yours alone. You are answerable for your half and not for theirs — which cuts both ways, and both edges are useful. It forbids carrying blame for what another person chose. And it forbids the far more common move of treating their bad conduct as a release from yours. WITH ALL MEN. Here the qualifications stop, and it is worth reading slowly because the exemptions we would want are precisely the ones not offered. Not the applicant who misrepresented his income. Not the tenant who left the unit damaged and the keys on the counter. Not the household currently in litigation against you. The two limits govern the OUTCOME; nothing whatsoever qualifies the SCOPE. SO DEFINE THE PORTION CONCRETELY, because as a sentiment this changes nothing on Monday. What lies in you: the call returned when you said you would; the number stated accurately, including the one that costs you; the deduction itemised and evidenced even when you know they will dispute it anyway; the deposit returned on the clock in an acrimonious exit; the notice given at the full stated period even when you are within your rights to be brisk; the repair completed to the standard you would want in your own home; the truth told about the property to the next applicant even when honesty costs a week of vacancy. What does NOT lie in you: their choices, the account they give of you to their friends, their conduct inside a dispute, and the outcome itself. Stop weighing yourself on that second column. AND SEE WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT ENDING FOR A LEASING COURSE, because the design is deliberate. Look back at the seven lessons before this one: telling the stranger what you know and they need; criteria written before the showing so partiality becomes visible; searching out the second account before deciding; reading the covenant aloud and asking for it back in their words; holding the pledge as a pledge and returning it before sundown; removing the things about you that make a household afraid; going privately and in meekness before going formal. NOT ONE OF THOSE REQUIRES THE TENANT TO COOPERATE. Every one of them lies entirely within your own hand. AND THERE IS ONE MORE THING TO SAY ABOUT DOING YOUR HALF FOR YEARS, because the failure is not usually a decision. It is erosion. "And let us not be weary in well doing: for in due season we shall reap, if we faint not" (Galatians 6:9). Weary is the word. Not corrupt, not cruel — tired. The landlord who stops returning the call, stops itemising, stops giving full notice, almost never chose any of that; he simply got tired of being the only one keeping the standard. IN DUE SEASON, and IF WE FAINT NOT: the harvest is promised and the condition is endurance. That is not a coincidence — it is the shape Romans 12:18 gives the whole trade: you are accountable for your portion, the portion is fully available to you today, and it is enough work for a lifetime.',
